Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hi all,

Background: I have been
working on developing some scales for the educational domain. Items are
categorical. Upon completion of the survey, I also want to run some tests of
measurement invariances. Here, I follow the procedure proposed by Wu and
Estabrook (2016). Hence, I estimate a baseline (configural) model, then a
threshold model and then a model on metric invariance (i.e., threshold and
loadings constrained to be equal across groups). To do so, I basically adjusted
the codes provided by Svetina et al. (2020). I had to adjust them to account for
the multiple imputations I compiled due to missingness in the data. As it is
common, to do so I used mice and thus run all MI models using the cfa.mi
command.

Problem: Executing
this procedure, I get results that I cannot explain let alone understand. In short,
the baseline and the thresholds models do not differ at all in terms of the fit
indices and even in terms of df. This is unexpected. Since in the latter model thresholds
are constrained to be equal across groups, the degrees of freedoms should go
up. I also run the same MI analysis for the factor model chosen across
different school subjects and my weird results replicate consistently. Basically,
my questions are what went wrong and how to fix it?

The implementation: The general
model chosen for the MI analysis is as follows:

library(lavaan)

library(semTools)

MODEL
<-  '

C_AB =~  C_AB2 + C_AB3 + C_AB4 + C_AB5 + C_AB6 + C_AB8

C_VA =~  C_DA1 + C_DA2 + C_DA3 + C_GE1 + C_GE2 + C_GE3
+ C_GE4 + C_GE5

C_CO =~  C_AB7 + C_EF2 + C_EF3 + C_EF5 + C_NE2 +
C_NE4'

the ordered
variables are:

VAR
<-   c("C_AB2",
"C_AB3", "C_AB4", "C_AB5", "C_AB6",
"C_AB8",

                  "C_DA1", "C_DA2",
"C_DA3", "C_GE1", "C_GE2", "C_GE3",
"C_GE4", "C_GE5",

                   "C_AB7", "C_EF2",
"C_EF3", "C_EF5", "C_NE2", "C_NE4")

As a first step, I used measEq.syntax
to generate the lavaan MI model syntax. This model was then estimated:

baseline <-
measEq.syntax(configural.model = MODEL,

                            ordered = VAR,

                            data = BASELINE_IMP_EX_1,

                            parameterization =
"delta",

                            ID.fac =
"std.lv <http://std.lv>",

                            ID.cat =
"Wu.Estabrook.2016",

                            group =
"Sex",

                            group.equal =
"configural")

model.baseline <-
as.character(baseline)

BASELINE.FIT <-
cfa.mi(model.baseline,

                         data=BASELINE_IMP_EX,

                         ordered = VAR,

                         group =
"Sex")

A comment on the usage of data in
both commands. As highlighted above, measEq.syntax and cfa.mi use different
data sources (BASELINE_IMP_EX_1
vs BASELINE_IMP_EX).
The latter data set is actually the imputed data that I compiled by the means
of using mice. The other data set is one of the individual datasets that were
compiled. I exported all imputed data sets to separate data frames because I
need them for something else. In general, one can use measEq.syntax without the
data add-on. Doing so, however, causes problems later on. Since one cannot
combine a mice mid object with measEq.syntax I used this ‘trick’. As a result,
I was able to estimate the baseline model.

Similarly, I proceeded to estimate
the threshold-invariance model:

prob4 <-
measEq.syntax(configural.model = MODEL,

                       data =
BASELINE_IMP_EX_1,

                       ordered = VAR,

                       parameterization =
"delta",

                       ID.fac =
"std.lv <http://std.lv>",

                       ID.cat =
"Wu.Estabrook.2016",

                       group.equal =
"thresholds",

                       group = "Sex")

model.prob4 <-
as.character(prob4)

PROB4.FIT <-
